  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Amit Kumar vs The State Of Madhya Pradesh on 14 February, 2020 Author: Sunil Kumar Awasthi Bench: Sunil Kumar Awasthi 1 The High Court Of Madhya Pradesh M.Cr.C.No.6784/2020 (Amit Kumar Vs. State of M.P.) Indore, dated :14/02/2020 Shri. Vinayak Balchandani, learned counsel for the applicant. Shri Gourav Verma, learned Public Prosecutor for the respondent / State . Heard.",
    "facts": "Applicant is apprehending her arrest in connection with Crime No. 12/2020 registered at Police-Station- Neemuch Cant., District-Neemuch, for the offence punishable under Sections 272, 273, 420 of I.P.C. As per prosecution case, on 03.01.2020 Food Safety Officer, Neemuch Mr. Sanjeev Kumar Mishra made a written complaint at police-station Neemuch Cant. stating that on 21.06.2019 he collected samples of coriander from the shop of present applicant and the sample of the said food article was sent to the Food Laboratory Bhopal and according to the report of Public Analysis the same was found sub-standard. The second sample was sent Referral Food Laboratory Mysore and the said report reflect that the sample does not conform to the standards laid down for Dhania Whole under the provision of Food Safety and Standards (Food Products Standards and Food Additives) Regulations 2011. Learned counsel for the applicant has submitted that the act of storing or selling adulterated article has been specifically punishable under the Food Safety & Standards Act, 2006 read with Food Safety and Standards (Food Products Standards and Food Additives) Regulations 2011 (in short \"Act 2006\") therefore, registration of FIR by the respondent under Section 272, 273 and 420 of I.P.C. is erroneous and if the sample taken from the shop is found adulterated then the Food Safety Officer is authorized to launch prosecution against the applicant under the Act, 2006. There are no ingredients available for constitution of offence under Section 420 of I.P.C. against the applicant and remaining offence under Section 272 and 273 are bailable in nature.",
    "arguments": "It is also submitted that the applicant is ready to co-operate with the investigation and there is no possibility of her absconsion Amit Kumar vs The State Of Madhya Pradesh on 14 February, 2020 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/119987057/ 1 or tampering with the evidence. Under these circumstances, learned counsel for the applicant prayed for grant of anticipatory bail to the applicant.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "It is also submitted that the applicant is ready to co-operate with the investigation and there is no possibility of her absconsion Amit Kumar vs The State Of Madhya Pradesh on 14 February, 2020 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/119987057/ 1 or tampering with the evidence. Under these circumstances, learned counsel for the applicant prayed for grant of anticipatory bail to the applicant.",
